    "ctgov_cond": "(\"Cardiovascular Disease\" OR \"Coronary Heart Disease\" OR \"Coronary Artery Disease\" OR \"Primary Prevention\" OR \"Cardiovascular Risk\" OR Hypercholesterolemia OR Hypercholesterolaemia OR Hyperlipidemia OR Hyperlipidaemia OR Dyslipidemia OR Dyslipidaemia OR Healthy)",
    "ctgov_intr": "(statin OR \"HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor\" OR \"HMG CoA reductase inhibitor\" OR \"hydroxymethylglutaryl-CoA reductase inhibitor\" OR atorvastatin OR rosuvastatin OR pravastatin OR simvastatin OR lovastatin OR fluvastatin OR pitavastatin OR cerivastatin OR mevastatin OR compactin OR Lipitor OR Sortis OR Tahor OR Torvast OR Atorvaliq OR Crestor OR Ezallor OR Pravachol OR Lipostat OR Selektine OR Zocor OR FloLipid OR Mevacor OR Altoprev OR Lescol OR Livalo OR Livazo OR Alipza OR Zypitamag OR Baycol OR Lipobay)",
    "rationale": "The PubMed strategy requires an older-adult concept, a prevention or prevention-relevant risk/population concept, and a statin concept, followed by the sensitivity-maximising Cochrane trial filter with an additional British-spelling term. Requiring both population components should improve precision relative to treating age and prevention as alternatives. Risk and lipid terms help retain parent reports and mixed populations whose eligible prevention subgroups are not described explicitly. MeSH Aged deliberately includes people younger than the eligibility threshold; exact age and absence of established CVD require screening. No outcome, date, language, named-trial, or separate comparator restriction is applied. Registry condition terms accommodate broader condition labels; age and prevention eligibility should be screened rather than represented by Elderly alone. The supplied text cannot establish the revised retrieval count: fewer than 9,000 records is a design target, not a verified result. Mandatory population wording/indexing can still miss incompletely described subgroup evidence, so retrieval validation is needed before registration.",
    "concepts": [
     "Older adults, using age-related MeSH headings and title/abstract synonyms",
     "Primary cardiovascular prevention, including disease-free populations and relevant risk or lipid-disorder descriptions",
     "Statins, using the class MeSH heading, class synonyms, generic drugs, and brand names",
     "Randomised trials, using the Cochrane sensitivity-maximising filter and animal-only exclusion"
    ],
    "weaknesses_of_current": [
     "Eligibility is unregistered, and the question's age threshold of >=70 conflicts with the population's >=75; this must be resolved before screening.",
     "Title requirements and unusually specific phrase combinations resemble known-report retrieval rather than a comprehensive, reproducible search.",
     "The PubMed searches cover only three statins and omit the class MeSH heading, other generic drugs, brand names, and class synonyms.",
     "Older-age wording is limited to specific phrases, without age MeSH headings or broader spelling and terminology variants.",
     "Requiring the exact phrase primary prevention can miss reports describing disease-free participants or cardiovascular risk without that phrase.",
     "Requiring Usual Care in a title can exclude eligible placebo or other control comparisons.",
     "There is no explicit randomised-trial filter.",
     "Separate narrow searches do not provide one coherent strategy covering the full review question.",
     "ClinicalTrials.gov Elderly is an unreliable condition proxy for age, and Atorvastatin excludes other statins.",
     "The reported retrieval counts do not demonstrate completeness or capture eligible subgroups within broader parent trials."
    ]
   },
